description:
Follow-up to bd-9ad9e5, which extracted and tested the ownership decision but deliberately did not convert the call sites.

`src/path-ownership.ts` now answers "may this uid own a path held under this policy", tested against every owner it must reject, and `test/path-ownership.test.mjs` pins which policy each module applies. The 43 guards themselves still open-code the comparison, in several shapes:

- the common form, `getuid !== undefined && info.uid !== getuid()`;
- the root-accepting form with `&& info.uid !== 0`;
- inline within a compound condition alongside symlink and mode checks, in `pi-adapter.ts` and `installed-package-resources.ts`;
- two-path forms comparing a socket and its parent directory, in `tool-adapter-runtime.ts`;
- `process.getuid` read inline rather than hoisted, in `self-update.ts`.

Converting them to `hasForeignPathOwner(info.uid, policy, process.getuid?.())` is mechanical but touches 22 security-critical modules, and an inversion in any one of them silently weakens a fail-closed check while every existing test still passes — precisely the vacuity problem the original bead was about. It therefore wants its own reviewable diff rather than riding along with the predicate.

Worth doing because the census only pins *counts and policies*, not that each site computes the decision correctly. Adoption is what makes the tested predicate authoritative rather than merely parallel to the guards.

Suggested approach: convert one module per commit or one shape per commit, keeping each error type and message byte-identical, and update the census in the same change so the count moves from open-coded to adopted rather than disappearing. A useful invariant to add at the end: no `.uid !== ` comparison remains in `src/` outside `path-ownership.ts`, which is checkable and would keep new guards on the helper.
